<h2>The case in one view</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Case</th><td>Sanjay Kumar v. State of NCT of Delhi, CRL.A. 647/2025</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Court</th><td>High Court of Delhi, Madhu Jain J</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Pronounced</th><td>6 November 2026 (reserved 2 November 2026)</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Provisions</th><td>Sections 304 Part II, 299, 323, 325, 341 IPC; appeal under Section 415(2) BNSS</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Outcome</th><td>Conviction altered to Section 325 IPC; sentence reduced to period undergone; fine maintained</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The deceased was admitted to a de-addiction centre in Delhi run by the appellant in July 2013. Five days later his brother came to take him home; the staff refused release until outstanding charges were paid, an altercation followed, and the brother&rsquo;s account was that the appellant dragged the deceased into a room and struck him in the abdomen. The deceased went to hospital that night complaining of abdominal pain; on a second visit hours later an assault history was recorded. He was moved through two more hospitals, underwent surgery for intestinal perforation, and died a week after the incident. The post-mortem attributed death to septicemic shock with blunt-force damage to abdominal structures. The trial court convicted the appellant under Section 304 Part II IPC in January 2025 and sentenced him to five years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment with a fine of Rs 50,000; a co-accused was acquitted.</p>

<h2>What stood: the assault and the causal chain</h2>
<p>The High Court sustained the finding that the appellant inflicted the abdominal injuries, resting on the brother&rsquo;s testimony and the medico-legal record, and held the death causally connected to those injuries. The eight-day interval, the inter-hospital transfers and the initial omission of an assault history did not break the chain; Explanation 2 to Section 299 IPC answers precisely the argument that proper treatment might have saved the victim. The court also accepted that, in principle, &ldquo;knowledge of the precise internal injuries is not an essential requirement of Section 304 Part II IPC.&rdquo;</p>

<h2>What fell: knowledge of likely death</h2>
<p>The alteration turned on the mental element. The trial court had inferred knowledge from the deceased&rsquo;s status as a person under addiction treatment; the High Court found that insufficient absent evidence that the appellant knew of any specific vulnerability. No weapon was used; the incident erupted suddenly out of a dispute over dues. What the evidence did establish was the intentional infliction of blows to the abdomen causing grievous injuries:</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;the evidence does establish that the appellant intentionally inflicted bodily injuries upon the deceased&rdquo; &mdash; and accordingly, &ldquo;The conviction deserves to be altered to one under Section 325 IPC.&rdquo;</p>
</div>
<p>On sentence, with about a year&rsquo;s imprisonment already undergone before suspension, the court reduced the substantive sentence to the period served, maintaining the fine and its default term, with release formalities subject to the nominal roll.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For the defence in assault-death cases.</strong> Causation and knowledge are separate battles. Conceding (or losing) the causal chain does not decide the Section 304 question; the realistic appellate target is often the knowledge inference, especially in sudden, weaponless altercations.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For prosecutors.</strong> If the theory is knowledge based on the victim&rsquo;s condition, prove the accused&rsquo;s awareness of it. The inference from occupation — running a treatment facility — was not enough here.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For institutions.</strong> The case began as a dispute over dues at a private de-addiction centre. Facilities that restrain or discipline residents operate one altercation away from criminal liability; protocols and training are cheaper than appeals.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>The gradation from Section 302 through Section 304 to the hurt offences is where Indian homicide law does its finest sorting, and appellate courts police the boundaries through exactly this kind of analysis: act proved, causation proved, mental element tested on its own evidence. The judgment also carries a quiet systemic note — the incident dates to 2013, the trial verdict to 2025, the appeal to 2026. Thirteen years from altercation to final characterisation is its own commentary on the docket, and a reminder that sentence arithmetic at the appellate stage often turns on custody already served.</p>
